(Regression test for bug #68: LANGUAGE.md ("Format Strings Everywhere")
 promises a format string renders identically in every sink, and bug #44
 made that true for a `{list}` variable part. A tagged EXPRESSION part
 never reached those arms - `emit_append_runtime_value_to_buffer_ptr` had
 no case for a value whose type is only known at runtime - so a text
 initializer, a buffer, a `write` and a function argument each got the
 integer formatter and stored an address.

 Acceptance: the same hole produces the same bytes in Print, in a text, in
 a fixed buffer, in a file read back from disk, and inside a function.)

To 'announce the text' with a text called line.
  Print line.

a list called nested is [1, [2, 3], "four"].

(Print position, the control.)
Print "{element 2 of nested} and {element 3 of nested}".

(A text initializer.)
a text called captured is "{element 2 of nested} and {element 3 of nested}".
Print captured.

(A fixed-size buffer.)
a buffer called sink is 128 bytes in size.
copy "{element 2 of nested} and {element 3 of nested}" to sink.
Print sink.

(A function argument.)
'announce the text' of "{element 2 of nested} and {element 3 of nested}".

(A real file, read back so the assertion is on the stored bytes.)
open a file for writing called record at "./test_bug68_write.txt".
Write "{element 2 of nested} and {element 3 of nested}\n" to record.
Close record.
open a file for reading called stored at "./test_bug68_write.txt".
a buffer called line is 256 bytes in size.
Read line from stored into line.
Print line without newline.
Close stored.
Delete the file "./test_bug68_write.txt".

(#81's other half, the same promise one specifier further on. A tagged
 NUMBER - a mixed list's integer element, a map read - carries its own
 runtime tag, and the buffer sinks have applied the hole's format spec to
 it since #68. Print's twin took no spec at all, so the same hole padded in
 a text and did not pad on screen. Both now agree. A tagged TEXT still
 ignores a width: there is no text-padding primitive behind the tag
 dispatch, which is #36's recorded residue and not this entry's to close.)
a list called sizes is [1, "two"].
a map called scores is {"ada": 10}.

a text called padded is "[{element 1 of sizes:5}]".
Print padded.
Print "[{element 1 of sizes:5}]".

a text called keyed is "[{scores's \"ada\":6}]".
Print keyed.
Print "[{scores's \"ada\":6}]".

Print "hex: {scores's \"ada\":x}".
Print "a tagged text ignores a width: [{element 2 of sizes:5}]".
